# Line-ending and diff policy for the turbo-grok-build workspace.
#
# Goal: the index is always LF, binaries are never touched, and every file that
# is baked into a shipped artifact has host-independent bytes.
#
# Rule ordering matters: within this file the LAST matching pattern wins, so the
# `* text=auto` catch-all below is deliberately first and every later section
# narrows it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Catch-all
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `text=auto` (NOT bare `text`, and NOT `text eol=lf`): git keeps applying its
# NUL-byte content heuristic, so a file it detects as binary is still left
# byte-for-byte alone. A bare `text` resolves to `text: set` — forced conversion
# with no detection — which would corrupt ~2.3 MB across the 9 binary assets
# below. Verified with `git check-attr text eol diff`.
* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary assets — belt and braces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The catch-all's heuristic already protects these, but stating them explicitly
# means the result does not *depend* on the heuristic. `binary` is git's alias
# for `-text -diff`.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed: *.glb. The only .glb files in this repo are five
# 7-byte text stubs under crates/codegen/xai-workspace-tree/tests/fixtures/,
# currently LF in the index and mixed in the worktree; marking them `binary`
# would freeze the corrupt worktree bytes into history. Real glTF binaries
# carry NUL bytes and are caught by the heuristic.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shipped POSIX executables — must be LF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `#!/bin/sh\r\n` first line makes the kernel reject the script with
# "bad interpreter". These reach end users, so they are pinned regardless of the
# platform the checkout happens on.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Extensionless shebang files (`#!/usr/bin/env dotslash` and `#!/usr/bin/env
# node`). Neither has an extension for a pattern to catch, so name them.
bin/protoc                                      text eol=lf
crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/grok/bin/grok text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-native executables — CRLF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PowerShell tolerates LF, but cmd.exe reads batch files incrementally and is
# documented to mis-handle multi-line constructs and `goto` labels when they are
# LF-terminated. These files are only ever executed by a Windows shell, so
# pinning CRLF costs nothing on unix and removes that hazard class outright.
# Pinned (not left to `text=auto`) so a Linux checkout produces the same bytes.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Markdown — LF everywhere
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not cosmetic. Markdown in this repo is compiled into the binary with
# `include_str!`/`include_bytes!`:
#   - crates/codegen/xai-grok-agent/templates/*.md are XOR-encrypted into
#     src/prompt/prompt_encrypted.rs, whose ciphertext is position-dependent;
#   - crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/templates/*.md are rendered at
#     runtime and asserted on at exact byte offsets;
#   - bundled/skills/**/*.md ship to end users.
# Under `text=auto` alone these are checked out CRLF on Windows and LF on unix,
# so the shipped system prompt would differ depending on which host ran the
# build. `eol=lf` makes the worktree bytes — and therefore the shipped bytes —
# the same everywhere.
*.md text eol=lf
